        <div id="indoor-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Indoor vs Outdoor Courts: The Difference That Matters More
          </h2>

          <p>
            The surface you play on matters more than the sport label on the shoe. Outdoor courts are usually asphalt or concrete with an acrylic coating, which is abrasive and wears outsoles relatively quickly. On those courts, a durable outsole with a tough rubber compound is worth more than a few saved ounces. Hard court tennis shoes and outdoor-focused pickleball shoes both suit this surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor pickleball is frequently played on gym floors, wood or synthetic, often shared with basketball and volleyball. Those floors are smooth, and the best grip often comes from softer, gum-rubber outsoles found on indoor court shoes, such as volleyball, badminton and some indoor pickleball shoes. A hard court tennis shoe can feel slippery on a dusty gym floor, especially as the outsole hardens with age.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you play mainly indoors, consider an indoor court shoe rather than either a tennis or an outdoor pickleball shoe. Keep those shoes for indoor use only, because walking outside in them picks up grit and wears the soft rubber, which reduces grip on the gym floor. If you split time between indoor and outdoor courts, two pairs is often the most practical answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ball changes too. Indoor and outdoor pickleballs differ in weight, hole pattern and bounce, which affects how fast rallies are and therefore how you move. Our explainer on <a href="/indoor-vs-outdoor-pickleball-balls/">indoor vs outdoor pickleball balls</a> covers those differences, and our guide to <a href="/pickleball-court-dimensions/">pickleball court dimensions</a> explains why the smaller court changes footwork compared with tennis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit-support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fit, Support and Foot Shape Matter More Than the Category
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important feature of any court shoe is how it fits your foot. A shoe that is too loose lets the foot slide inside during lateral cuts, which reduces control and can cause blisters. A shoe that is too tight in the toe box can cause pain during long sessions and may contribute to toenail problems from repeated stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width is a common issue. Many players with wider feet find that some tennis lines offer wide versions in more sizes than some newer pickleball lines. If you have a wide or narrow foot, start with the brands and models that offer your width, and choose between tennis and pickleball versions from there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Support needs vary. Players with a history of ankle sprains, flat feet or knee problems may benefit from shoes with firmer heel counters and more midfoot structure, and some may use orthotic inserts. Make sure any shoe you choose has a removable insole if you use custom or over-the-counter orthotics, and bring them when you try shoes on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try shoes on later in the day when feet are slightly larger, wear the socks you play in, and test lateral movements in the store if you can. Hop side to side, plant and push off. If your heel lifts or your foot slides over the sole edge, try another model, whatever the label says.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When a Dedicated Pickleball Shoe Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pickleball shoe is worth buying if you play pickleball several times a week, rarely play tennis, and want a shoe tuned to the faster, shorter movements of the pickleball court. Players who spend most of their time at the kitchen line often appreciate a lighter shoe that responds quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also makes sense if you have tried tennis shoes and found them heavier or stiffer than you like. Lighter pickleball models can feel more agile, and some players find them more comfortable over long open-play sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            A tennis shoe is the better choice if you play both sports, if you need a width or size that pickleball lines do not offer, or if you want the reassurance of a long-established model with a durability guarantee. It can also be the cheaper option, because tennis models from previous seasons are frequently discounted.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own good tennis shoes, use them until they wear out and then decide. There is no need to replace a pair that fits and supports you just because the box does not say pickleball. When the time comes, our <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">pickleball shoe picks</a> cover dedicated models alongside court shoes that cross over well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Choose Either One
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the surface. For outdoor hard courts, prioritize a durable rubber outsole and, if you play often, consider models with outsole durability guarantees. For indoor gym floors, look at indoor court shoes with softer, grippier rubber and keep them for indoor use only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, choose fit and width. Shop from brands that offer your width, try shoes on with your playing socks and any orthotics, and test lateral movement. A secure heel and a midfoot that holds the foot over the sole matter more than any technology name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then think about weight and cushioning. If you value quick feet and play mostly near the net, a lighter shoe may suit you. If you have joint concerns or play long sessions, prioritize cushioning and stability, which may point you to a sturdier tennis model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, replace shoes when they wear. Worn outsoles lose grip and worn midsoles lose support, which raises the risk of slips and rolled ankles. Many players find that frequent outdoor play wears an outsole through within a season. Pair good shoes with a paddle that suits your game, our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> guide covers the options, and you have most of the equipment questions settled.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Playing in running shoes instead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The real mistake is not choosing tennis over pickleball shoes but choosing running shoes over either. Running shoes are built for forward motion, with high heels and soft foam that can make lateral cuts unstable. Any well-fitting court shoe is a better choice for pickleball.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using outdoor shoes on indoor gym floors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard court outsoles that grip well on gritty outdoor courts can feel slippery on smooth, dusty gym floors. If you play mostly indoors, an indoor court shoe with softer gum rubber usually grips better, and keeping it for indoor use preserves that grip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the label instead of the fit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pickleball-branded shoe that slides on your foot is worse than a tennis shoe that fits well. Choose the model that holds your heel and midfoot securely and suits your width, then worry about whether it says tennis or pickleball.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring worn outsoles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Outdoor courts are abrasive, and worn outsoles lose grip and stability before the uppers look old. Check the tread under the ball of the foot and the toe regularly, and replace shoes once the pattern is worn smooth rather than waiting for a slip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a lateral test when trying on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Walking around a store tells you little about a court shoe. Shuffle side to side, plant hard and push off in the shoes before buying. If your heel lifts or your foot rolls toward the edge of the sole, keep looking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you wear tennis shoes for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tennis shoes are designed for the same lateral movements, quick stops and hard court surfaces as pickleball, and they are one of the best choices for the sport. Many players use tennis shoes for years. Choose a pair that fits well and suits your court surface, and replace them when the outsole wears smooth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pickleball shoes different from tennis shoes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Somewhat. Both are court shoes with lateral support and multidirectional outsoles. Pickleball shoes are often a little lighter and tuned for the shorter, quicker steps of the smaller court, while some tennis shoes are heavier and built for long baseline rallies and heavy toe drag. The differences are modest compared with running shoes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pickleball shoes work for tennis?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes for casual tennis, since both are court shoes. Heavier tennis players or those who drag their toes hard during serves may find some lighter pickleball shoes wear faster or offer less support during long rallies. If tennis is your main sport, a tennis-specific shoe is the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What shoes are best for indoor pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Indoor court shoes with softer, gum-rubber outsoles usually grip best on smooth gym floors. Volleyball, badminton and indoor-specific pickleball shoes fall into this category. Keep them for indoor use only, because outdoor grit wears the soft rubber and reduces grip on the gym floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do pickleball and tennis shoes last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often and where you play. Outdoor hard courts are abrasive, and many regular players wear through an outsole within a season or two. Indoor shoes kept off outdoor surfaces tend to last longer. Replace shoes when the tread is worn smooth or the midsole no longer feels supportive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pickleball shoes worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you play pickleball often and rarely play tennis, a dedicated pickleball shoe can be worth it for its lighter, quicker feel. If you play both sports or already own good tennis shoes, there is no need to buy a separate pair. Fit and court surface matter more than the label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I wear cross-trainers for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cross-trainers are a reasonable compromise for occasional players because they are flatter and more laterally stable than running shoes. They usually lack the durable, court-specific outsoles of tennis and pickleball shoes, so regular players are better served by a proper court shoe.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

